Subject: [PATCH] nvme-pci: log pci state around flr recovery

Debug only, not for submission.  Applies on top of
0070-nvme-pci-restore-config-space-after-late-device-reset.patch.

When the FLR fallback leaves the register window decoding nothing, the logs
say only that the restore did not stick.  They do not say whether the
controller was answering config requests at the time, which is the
difference between a controller that is still initialising and one that has
left the bus, and so the difference between a host timing problem and a
device that does not implement FLR to spec.

Sample the endpoint's Vendor ID, command register and BAR0 together with
the upstream root port's link and device status at the three points that
matter: as soon as pcie_flr() returns, on entry to the config space restore
retry, and if that retry gives up.

The Vendor ID is the field to read first.  With Configuration RRS Software
Visibility enabled on the root port, pci_bus_rrs_vendor_id() matches a low
half of 0x0001, so that value means the controller responded with Request
Retry Status: it is not ready yet but it is completing config requests as
PCIe r6.x sec 6.6.2 requires.  ~0 means it is not completing them at all.
Reading the root port then tells the two causes apart, because Data Link
Layer Link Active clear in its link status means the controller dropped the
link, which an FLR is not permitted to do, while a live link points at
requests being discarded instead.

Also count the restore attempts and report them, so the log shows how long
the controller stayed unreachable rather than only that it came back.

---
diff -Nur a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
@@ -2670,6 +2670,35 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ * Diagnostic for post-FLR recovery.  The Vendor ID is the interesting field:
+ * with Configuration RRS Software Visibility enabled on the root port, a low
+ * half of 0x0001 means the controller answered Request Retry Status, i.e. it
+ * is still initialising but is completing config requests as the spec
+ * requires.  ~0 instead means it is not completing them at all, which it may
+ * only do while an FLR is still in progress.  The root port's link status
+ * tells a dropped link apart from requests being discarded on a live link.
+ */
+static void nvme_pci_log_reset_state(struct nvme_dev *dev, const char *when)
+{
+	struct pci_dev *pdev = to_pci_dev(dev->dev);
+	struct pci_dev *bridge = pci_upstream_bridge(pdev);
+	u16 cmd, lnksta = 0, devsta = 0;
+	u32 vid, bar0;
+
+	pci_read_config_dword(pdev, PCI_VENDOR_ID, &vid);
+	pci_read_config_word(pdev, PCI_COMMAND, &cmd);
+	pci_read_config_dword(pdev, PCI_BASE_ADDRESS_0, &bar0);
+	if (bridge) {
+		pcie_capability_read_word(bridge, PCI_EXP_LNKSTA, &lnksta);
+		pcie_capability_read_word(bridge, PCI_EXP_DEVSTA, &devsta);
+	}
+
+	dev_info(dev->ctrl.device,
+		 "flr diag %s: vid %#010x cmd %#06x bar0 %#010x rp lnksta %#06x devsta %#06x\n",
+		 when, vid, cmd, bar0, lnksta, devsta);
+}
+
+/*
  * A controller can keep re-applying its reset defaults after pcie_flr() has
  * seen config space start responding again, dropping the values the FLR
  * fallback restored.  That leaves the BARs unassigned, so the register window
@@ -2685,8 +2714,11 @@
 static int nvme_pci_restore_config_space(struct nvme_dev *dev)
 {
 	struct pci_dev *pdev = to_pci_dev(dev->dev);
+	unsigned int attempts = 0;
 	unsigned long settle;
 
+	nvme_pci_log_reset_state(dev, "csts unreadable");
+
 	if (!pdev->state_saved)
 		return -ENODEV;
 
@@ -2694,15 +2726,19 @@
 	for (;;) {
 		pci_restore_state(pdev);
 		pdev->state_saved = true;
+		attempts++;
 
 		if (readl(dev->bar + NVME_REG_CSTS) != -1) {
 			dev_warn(dev->ctrl.device,
-				 "registers responded after restoring config space\n");
+				 "registers responded after restoring config space (%u attempts)\n",
+				 attempts);
 			return 0;
 		}
 
-		if (pci_dev_is_disconnected(pdev) || time_after(jiffies, settle))
+		if (pci_dev_is_disconnected(pdev) || time_after(jiffies, settle)) {
+			nvme_pci_log_reset_state(dev, "gave up");
 			return -ENODEV;
+		}
 
 		msleep(100);
 	}
@@ -2852,6 +2888,12 @@
 					 "pcie flr fallback failed (%d)\n",
 					 result);
 			} else {
+				/*
+				 * Sample the state pci_dev_wait() decided was
+				 * ready, before the restore does any config
+				 * access of its own.
+				 */
+				nvme_pci_log_reset_state(dev, "flr returned");
 				pci_restore_state(pdev);
 				/*
 				 * Keep the saved copy armed so that
